<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="coverlet.collector" Version="6.0.4" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing" Version="10.0.11" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="17.14.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit" Version="2.9.3" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="3.1.4" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\LandMoney.Web\LandMoney.Web.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    OwnershipFilterTests uses EF Core as of #52; before that nothing here did.
    This reference exists to stop 66 MSB3277 warnings, and the reason is worth
    writing down, because the fix looks arbitrary otherwise.

    Bumped 10.0.10 -> 10.0.11 in #52, and the mechanism is worth recording
    because the note below predicted it and it still cost a red build. Adding
    Microsoft.AspNetCore.Identity.EntityFrameworkCore 10.0.11 to LandMoney.Web
    raised the EF graph there by one patch; this project still asked for 10.0.10,
    and NuGet reports that as
    "error NU1605: Detected package downgrade: Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Relational
    from 10.0.11 to 10.0.10", which fails restore rather than warning. So the
    prediction was right about the drift and wrong about how loudly it arrives:
    the warnings did not come back, an error did. That is the better outcome.

    This is the second time this file has had to be edited to follow a version
    it does not use. The third is when Directory.Packages.props stops being
    theoretical.

    LandMoney.Web references Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Design with
    PrivateAssets="all", which is right: it is a design-time tool for
    `dotnet ef` and has no business in anything downstream. Design 10.0.10 is
    also what raises the whole EF graph to 10.0.10 in that project, because
    Npgsql.EntityFrameworkCore.PostgreSQL 10.0.3 asks only for 10.0.4. Private
    assets do not flow, so this project resolves 10.0.4 while the LandMoney.Web
    assembly it references was compiled against 10.0.10.

    That is a warning today and a FileLoadException the first time a test touches
    EF, since assembly binding rolls forward to a higher version and never down.
    Pinning Relational is enough: it drags Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore and
    .Abstractions up with it.

    The real answer is Directory.Packages.props, central package management, one
    PackageVersion per package for the whole repository. That was not worth a
    repository-wide change inside #21 for two projects, and it is the thing to
    reach for the moment there is a third. Until then this version has to be kept
    equal to the one LandMoney.Web resolves. If it drifts, the warnings come back
    and say so, which is the whole reason for not suppressing them instead.

    (An MSBuild comment is XML, so the double dash this repository writes prose
    with is a parse error inside one. Learned by writing this comment.)
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Relational" Version="10.0.11" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Using Include="Xunit" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
